hello everyone and welcome to mad  simplified i'm dr umar malik and today  we are going to talk about a few  protein-rich foods which can be included  in a vegan diet  but first let's talk about veganism  veganism is the practice of abstaining  from the use of animal products  particularly in the diet and an  associated philosophy that rejects the  commodity status of animals in general  an individual who follows such a diet or  the philosophy is known as a vegan  now there are different categories under  which veganism can be divided there are  dietary vegans ethical vegans and  environmental vegans  the dietary vegans are also known as  strict vegetarians as they completely  refrain from consuming meat eggs dairy  products and other animal dried products  a person can be called as an ethical  vegan who not only follows plant-based  diet but extends their vegan philosophy  into other areas of their life and  opposes the use of animals for any other  purposes altogether  now there is one other type of veganism  known as environmental veganism which  simply refers to the avoidance of any  type of animal products and they are  byproducts on the premise that the  industrial farming of animals is  environmentally damaging and  unsustainable  a well-planned vegan diet is regarded as  appropriate for all stages of life  including infancy and pregnancy as said  by the american academy of nutrition and  dietetics  it is considered to be high in dietary  fiber magnesium folic acid vitamin c  vitamin e iron and other phytochemicals  now i'm sure you must have realized that  there is one very important  macronutrient missing here and that is  the proteins  the building block for your bones  muscles cartilage and skin  in fact your hair and nails are also  comprised mostly of proteins as well  now a person can obtain protein from  various sources like meat eggs and  yogurt but this can't be the case in  terms of a vegan diet  most vegan foods have significantly low  amount of protein as a result of which  there can be a serious deficiency of  proteins in our body  a deficiency of protein can lead to  several complications including loss of  muscle mass high risk of infections  fatty liver  inhibited body growth  increased risk of bone fractures skin  hair and nail problems  bigger appetite and increased calorie  intake which can lead to weight gain  so as veganism is gaining popularity  around the globe it is essential to keep  track of your macronutrients so that  your body can function properly and  disease free  here are a few foods that vegans can  include in their diet which are high in  protein  number one  edamame tofu and tempeh  tofu tempeh and edamame all originate  from soy beans and soybean itself is  considered as a whole source of protein  and is not a derivation of animal  product  these three are capable of providing a  body with all the essential amino acids  and these amino acids are the building  blocks of proteins  they are made up of hundreds or  thousands of smaller amino acids which  are attached to one another in long  chains thus forming a protein  adamame basically are immature soy beans  with a sweet and slightly grassy taste  once boiled they can be easily added to  soups and salads or they can be consumed  or their own  100 grams of cooked edamame can provide  11 grams of protein which can be a good  contribution to your daily recommended  intake of protein  a is also rich in folate vitamin k and  fiber which can help to improve  digestion and overall health  tofu is made from dried soy beans which  are soaked in water crushed and then  boiled  it doesn't have much taste on its own  but it can easily absorb the flavor of  ingredients it is prepared with  100 grams of tofu can provide 8 grams of  protein whereas tempeh is a traditional  indonesian food made from fermented soy  beans  it is made by natural culturing and  controlled fermentation process that  binds soybeans into cake form  100 grams of tempeh can provide with a  staggering 19 grams of protein  it also contains probiotics b vitamins  and minerals such as magnesium and  phosphorus which are really important  for our body  number two  oats and oatmeal  along with several other benefits that  oats provide such as lowering the blood  sugar levels providing antioxidants  promoting healthy gut bacteria and even  lowering the chance of colon cancer they  are a potential low cost source of  protein with good nutritional value  100 grams of oats can provide around 13  to 14 grams of protein which can easily  contribute to your daily recommended  intake of protein  not only this oats are very easy to  prepare and require very little effort  they also contain magnesium zinc  phosphorus and folate which are really  important to maintain a healthy chemical  balance in our body  you can use oats in a variety of recipes  ranging from oatmeal to veg burgers  they can also be grounded into floor and  used for baking  number three quinoa quinoa is a seed  that you can find in white  red black or mixed varieties 100 gram of  quinoa will provide almost 4 grams of  protein  and it's a complete source of protein  which means it contains all essential  amino acids making it a great  alternative to carbohydrates such as  rice and couscous  along with routine it is high in  important vitamins and minerals such as  potassium iron folate and many more  to know more about quinoa check out our  video on quinoa and its health benefits  the links are in the description box  down below  number four  peanuts and peanut butter  nuts and their delight products are  great sources of protein  peanuts are a rich source of protein  fats and fiber also  their protein content ranges from 22  percent to 30 percent of their total  calories  being a plant food it is an  exceptionally good source of protein  just 100 grams of peanut butter can  easily provide you with 25 grams of  protein which is a lot and greatly  contributes to your daily recommended  protein intake  you can eat peanut butter on its own but  you have to develop a taste for it or  you can simply spread it on some bread  and pieces of banana and you have a high  protein and fiber sandwich  or the traditional peanut butter and  jelly sandwich can always do the trick  but be aware of the added sugars in the  jelly  today there are several flavored peanut  butters available in the market which  can make your food delicious as well as  nutritious at the same time  but keep this in mind peanuts have two  types of proteins known as arachin and  conoracans and some people can be  deathly allergic to them so be aware  number five protein-rich fruits and  vegetables  although all fruits and vegetables  contain protein  some contain more than others  vegetables with the most amount of  protein include broccoli spinach  asparagus artichokes potatoes and  brussels sprouts  which typically contain four to five  grams of proteins per cooked cup  fresh fruits generally have a lower  protein content than vegetables  those containing the most include guava  cherry moyas mulberries blackberries  nectarines and bananas which have about  2-4 grams of protein  per cup  so friends with this we have reached the  end of this video we hope these protein  rich vegan foods will be of great  benefit to you and you can easily  incorporate them into your diet  we at med simplified are committed to  bringing you the latest and updated  content related to health diet and  wellness  we also have an instagram page where we  post daily tidbits related to diet and  supplementation along with several  surprising facts  check it out i think you will definitely  like it  if you are new to our channel consider  subscribing you can support us via  patreon and on youtube  take care bye bye and i'll see you in  the next video
 

